<article class="env-lemma" id="00E5">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00E5">Lemma <span data-tag="00E5">10.17.7</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $I \subset R$ be an ideal. The map $R \to R/I$ induces via the functoriality of $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}$ a homeomorphism </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R/I) \longrightarrow V(I) \subset \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R).  \]
</div>
  <p> The inverse is given by $\mathfrak p \mapsto \mathfrak p / I$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It is immediate that the image is contained in $V(I)$. On the other hand, if $\mathfrak p \in V(I)$ then $\mathfrak p \supset I$ and we may consider the ideal $\mathfrak p /I \subset R/I$. Using basic notion (<a href="/tag/00C3" data-tag="00C3">51</a>) we see that $(R/I)/(\mathfrak p/I) = R/\mathfrak p$ is a domain and hence $\mathfrak p/I$ is a prime ideal. From this it is immediately clear that the image of $D(f + I)$ is $D(f) \cap V(I)$, and hence the map is a homeomorphism. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>